// ================================================================================================
// appsettings.json - the DEFAULT CONFIGURATION CONTRACT of the DataServices service.
// ================================================================================================
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS
// The base settings every DataServices deployment starts from. It is one half of a two-part
// contract: Configuration/DataServicesOptions.cs declares the shape and the types, this file
// supplies the values, and Program.cs binds the two with startup validation. The two halves must
// agree KEY FOR KEY. A key here with no property there binds to nothing and is silently ignored; a
// property there with no key here is an undeclared default that no operator can discover from the
// configuration document. Both are defects, so both directions were reconciled - see RECONCILIATION
// at the foot of this header.
//
// WHY THIS FILE CARRIES COMMENTS, AND WHY THAT IS SAFE
// Strict RFC 8259 JSON has no comment syntax, but the .NET JSON configuration provider tolerates
// both `//` line and slash-star block comments. VERIFIED ON THIS TOOLCHAIN rather than assumed: a
// file of exactly this shape was loaded through AddJsonFile on SDK 10.0.302 with
// Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Json 10.0.10 and parsed without an exception. Constraint C-K
// requires every technology-specific and boundary-specific decision to be documented, and a value
// annotated where it lives is far harder to change by accident than one annotated elsewhere - so
// every preserved legacy default below names the ws_objects/** line it was read from, and every
// non-obvious choice states its reason. The identical annotations also exist as XML documentation on
// Configuration/DataServicesOptions.cs, so nothing is recorded in only one place.
//
// ONE CONSEQUENCE FOR ANY OTHER CONSUMER OF THIS FILE. A reader that parses it with
// System.Text.Json directly must pass JsonCommentHandling.Skip; ConfigurationBuilder.AddJsonFile
// already does. A `jq` or strict-JSON-schema pass over this path would need the same allowance.
//
// RULES POSITION, STATED PLAINLY
// The project's rules document was retrieved and contains exactly one statement: no user rules were
// provided. That is a finding, not latitude, and nothing is invented in their place. The binding
// constraints are the twelve non-rule constraints C-A through C-L of the transformation plan
// together with its enterprise baseline. Those that govern THIS file:
//
//   C-B  no behaviour improvements. Every preserved legacy default below is the EXACT legacy value
//        with its locator. Three are easy to get silently wrong and are called out at their keys:
//        DropDownSearch.FilterType is 3 and not 7, DropDownSearch.ShowFilteredRows is null and not
//        false, and ColumnExpression.RedrawSuppressionRowThreshold is exactly 200.
//   C-E  no fabricated database. There is NO connection string, NO data-source key, NO provider
//        key and NO database section anywhere in this file, and none may be added. DataServices
//        holds no storage provider; it reaches data solely over the persistence.v1 gRPC contract,
//        addressed by DataServices:Persistence:Address and by nothing else.
//   C-F  nothing hardcoded may be carried forward, and the three named secret sites are a floor
//        rather than a ceiling. THERE IS NOT ONE SECRET VALUE IN THIS FILE. No signing key, no
//        password, no bearer token, no PEM block, no base64 DER, no certificate, no certificate
//        path, no API key - not as a value, not as a placeholder and not as an example. The only
//        key name below containing the word "key" is ValidateIssuerSigningKey, which is a boolean
//        switch and could not hold material even if someone tried.
//   C-G  no new attack surface: every newly created boundary is authenticated, and Security is the
//        SOLE issuer. The Authentication:Jwt section is VERIFICATION configuration only. All four
//        Validate* switches are true and none may be weakened. There is no signing property of any
//        name and no reference to Security's signing variable, which belongs to Security alone.
//   C-J  independently deployable. Every value here is either a compose-network address or a
//        behavioural default; nothing couples this service to a sibling's internals.
//   C-K  document every technology-specific and boundary-specific decision. Hence these comments.
//   C-L  the attached environment's operational constraints are binding: this service listens on
//        5102 inside the 5101-5105 band, /health is anonymous so the Compose health chain can gate
//        Gateway behind it, and /v1/ping requires a token and answers 401 without one.
//
// NO PERFORMANCE OBJECTIVE IS ASSERTED ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE. The repository publishes no
// service-level agreement, no latency budget, no throughput target and no availability commitment,
// so none may be claimed, met or used to justify a value. The failure-handling numbers under
// DataServices:Resilience exist because an in-process call cannot fail in transit and a network call
// can; the row threshold under DataServices:ColumnExpression is a reproduced legacy behaviour with
// an observable boundary. Neither is a target.
//
// THREE SECTIONS ARE BOUND BY THE FRAMEWORK RATHER THAN BY DataServicesOptions.cs, and each is
// named here so that the key-for-key rule has no apparent orphan: `Logging` binds
// LoggerFilterOptions, `AllowedHosts` binds HostFilteringOptions, and `Kestrel` binds
// KestrelServerOptions - which is also where this service's two listen addresses live, for the
// reason recorded on that section. The two sections DataServicesOptions.cs owns are
// `Authentication:Jwt` (JwtAuthenticationOptions) and `DataServices` (DataServicesOptions).
//
// THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO TOP-LEVEL `Urls` KEY. This service listens on two ports with a different
// protocol version on each, and `Urls` carries addresses with no protocol association, so it cannot
// express that at all. See the Kestrel section for the measured constraint that forces the split and
// for the environment keys that override each address.
//
// RECONCILIATION AGAINST Configuration/DataServicesOptions.cs - both directions, zero orphans.
// 34 leaf keys under `DataServices`, 8 under `Authentication:Jwt`, 42 in total, one per property.
// Three points where the shape is not the obvious one, recorded because each is a trap:
//
//   1. THE TWO SESSION GROUPS ARE NESTED UNDER `DataServices:Sessions:`, not at the `DataServices`
//      root. The property is DataServicesOptions.Sessions of type SessionsOptions, whose two
//      members are ValidationSession and ExpressionSession. Writing DataServices:ValidationSession
//      would bind nothing at all and leave the real values on their compiled-in defaults.
//   2. `DataServices:DropDownSearch:PinyinMatchFlags` EXISTS and is 7. It is a third preserved
//      legacy default in that group, distinct from FilterType, and omitting it would leave the one
//      value in this file whose numeral is genuinely 7 undeclared.
//   3. THERE IS NO BACKOFF-TYPE KEY, deliberately. ClientResilienceOptions exposes
//      MaxRetryAttempts, RetryBaseDelay, four circuit-breaker members and RequestTimeout, and no
//      backoff-type member; the base delay is the only backoff input the options type takes. A
//      `BackoffType` key here would bind to nothing, which is precisely the orphan this
//      reconciliation exists to prevent, so the backoff shape stays where it is chosen, in
//      Clients/PersistenceClient.cs and Clients/SecurityClient.cs.
//
// ================================================================================================
{
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // LOGGING - the built-in abstractions from the shared framework, and nothing else.
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Serilog and the OpenTelemetry family are on the deliberate-exclusion list and are absent by
  // design, not by oversight: the shared framework's own logging abstractions cover the
  // requirement, and neither package is in the mandated stack. The project file records the same
  // omission from the other direction.
  //
  // THIS SERVICE CARRIES A REDACTION OBLIGATION, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS SECTION HAS A COMMENT AT
  // ALL. DataServices relays the structured database error of the persistence.v1 contract, whose
  // statement field carries the COMPLETE GENERATED SQL INCLUDING INTERPOLATED LITERAL VALUES - the
  // legacy transaction layer parses its connection string for a bind-disabling flag
  // [ws_objects/pfw.thread.ext.pbl.src/n_cst_thread_task_sqlbase.sru:L128-L129], and with binding
  // disabled the runtime interpolates literals rather than using bind variables. The legacy logger
  // performed no redaction whatsoever. No log record emitted by this service may reproduce that
  // field unredacted, and no log level configured here grants permission to: a level decides
  // WHETHER a record is written, never whether its content is safe. The same caution applies to
  // expression text and caret positions in column-expression parse errors, which quote
  // caller-supplied expressions verbatim.
  //
  // Raising `Default` to Debug or Trace in a deployment therefore does not relax that obligation.
  // The development overlay raises levels for developer runs; it does not and must not disable
  // redaction.
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      // The service's own records. Information is the level at which lifecycle, session and
      // event-chain records are written.
      "Default": "Information",
      // Framework request logging. Warning keeps a per-request record out of the log while leaving
      // every fault visible - and it matters here because the Compose health probe polls /health
      // continuously, so Information would bury real records under readiness traffic.
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning",
      // The two typed clients, Clients/PersistenceClient.cs and Clients/SecurityClient.cs. Their
      // request records include the request URI, which is why no address in this file may ever
      // carry credentials - the address validator rejects a userinfo component for exactly this
      // reason.
      "System.Net.Http.HttpClient": "Warning",
      // The gRPC server for contracts C-03 and C-04 and the client for C-05 through C-08.
      "Grpc": "Warning"
    }
  },

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // HOST FILTERING - bound by the framework's HostFilteringOptions.
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Left open deliberately. Inside the orchestration network this service is reached by its service
  // name, from the Compose health probe by whatever name that probe uses, and from a developer run
  // over loopback; a host allow-list would reject one of those three and the failure would present
  // as an unexplained 400 on a readiness probe. Host filtering is not the control that protects
  // this service - every route other than the anonymous /health requires a bearer credential
  // verified against Security's published material (C-G), which is a control on the caller's
  // identity rather than on a header the caller chooses.
  "AllowedHosts": "*",

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // KESTREL - ONE TLS-TERMINATED ENDPOINT ON THE DOCUMENTED PORT, CARRYING BOTH PROTOCOL VERSIONS.
  // Read this section before changing the address or the scheme: both are forced by a measured
  // platform constraint together with the fixed port map, not chosen for taste.
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // THIS IS THE MOST LOAD-BEARING TECHNICAL SETTING IN THE FILE. Two kinds of traffic reach this
  // service and they do not speak the same protocol version:
  //
  //   * gRPC REQUIRES HTTP/2. Contracts C-03 (DataWindowService) and C-04
  //     (ColumnExpressionService) are gRPC, and C-04 carries two inverted streams, so HTTP/2 is not
  //     optional for them.
  //   * REST /health AND /v1/ping ARE PROBED WITH HTTP/1.1. The Compose health check shells out to
  //     curl, whose default is HTTP/1.1, and the readiness chain that gates Gateway behind this
  //     service depends on that probe answering (C-L). The end-to-end suite probes /health the same
  //     way through its own HTTP/1.1 client.
  //
  // BOTH MUST THEREFORE SHARE PORT 5102, BECAUSE THE PORT MAP FIXES ONE PORT PER SERVICE. AAP
  // 0.3.2.2 and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md 4.1 assign this service exactly 5102 and describe it as
  // carrying gRPC AND the thin REST projection; 5103 is the reserved Phase-2 DesignSystem slot and
  // must stay reserved (C-D), and the band 5101-5105 is otherwise fully allocated. Inventing an
  // undeclared port outside that band is not available: it contradicts the fixed contract, and it
  // leaves every caller with two addresses for one service to keep in step - which is precisely how
  // a caller ends up pointed at the half that cannot serve it.
  //
  // THE CLEARTEXT SINGLE-PORT CONFIGURATION DOES NOT WORK, AND THIS WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN
  // ASSUMED, TWICE, ON SDK 10.0.302. A single CLEARTEXT endpoint with Protocols "Http1AndHttp2" was
  // configured, started and probed. Kestrel logged, at warning level:
  //
  //     HTTP/2 is not enabled for [::]:5102. The endpoint is configured to use HTTP/1.1 and
  //     HTTP/2, but TLS is not enabled. HTTP/2 requires TLS application protocol negotiation.
  //     Connections to this endpoint will use HTTP/1.1.
  //
  // and the probes agreed with the warning: HTTP/1.1 GET /health returned 200, while an HTTP/2
  // prior-knowledge request to the same port failed with `curl: (16) Remote peer returned
  // unexpected data while we expected SETTINGS frame`. On a cleartext listener Kestrel does not fall
  // back to prior-knowledge negotiation when both versions are configured - it disables HTTP/2
  // outright. The mirror-image measurement: a cleartext endpoint with Protocols "Http2" alone
  // served HTTP/2 successfully and answered a plain HTTP/1.1 `GET /health` with 400, so that
  // configuration loses the readiness probe instead of losing gRPC.
  //
  // TLS IS THE RESOLUTION, AND IT IS FUNCTIONAL RATHER THAN A HARDENING PREFERENCE. Also measured on
  // the same toolchain: a TLS endpoint with Protocols "Http1AndHttp2" served BOTH versions on ONE
  // port - `curl --http1.1` returned 200 over HTTP/1.1 and `curl --http2` returned 200 over HTTP/2,
  // against the same listener - because ALPN is available to negotiate and the ambiguity that
  // disables HTTP/2 on cleartext does not arise. That is the only configuration in which the port
  // map, gRPC and the HTTP/1.1 readiness probe are simultaneously satisfiable.
  //
  // The alternatives were each rejected for a stated reason:
  //   * A second endpoint on an undeclared port for h2c - contradicts the fixed port map above, and
  //     forces every caller to hold two addresses for one service.
  //   * Protocols "Http2" alone on 5102 - measured above: the readiness probe stops answering, so
  //     the chain gating Gateway could never satisfy.
  //   * Moving /health or /v1/ping off 5102 - forbidden by C-L, which fixes both there.
  //   * Dropping a protocol - never an option.
  //
  // NO CERTIFICATE, NO KEY AND NO PASSPHRASE APPEARS IN THIS SECTION, AND THE SCHEME DOES NOT
  // REQUIRE ONE TO. Kestrel resolves an HTTPS endpoint's certificate in a fixed order: an explicit
  // certificate on the endpoint, then `Kestrel:Certificates:Default`, then the local ASP.NET Core
  // development certificate, and it FAILS TO START if none is found. So the material is supplied by
  // the orchestration secret layer as environment configuration - Kestrel__Certificates__Default__
  // Path and __KeyPath, pointing at files mounted read-only into the container - and its absence is
  // a fail-fast startup error rather than a silent downgrade to plaintext. Nothing here holds a
  // path, a store reference or a password (C-F), and the surest way not to acquire one is to have
  // nowhere in this file to put it.
  //
  // WHY THE ADDRESS LIVES HERE RATHER THAN ON A TOP-LEVEL `Urls` KEY. `Urls` carries addresses with
  // no protocol association, so it cannot express Protocols at all, and Protocols is the whole point
  // of this entry. One consequence follows and is stated plainly: Kestrel binds CONFIGURED ENDPOINTS
  // in preference to host addresses whenever PreferHostingUrls is false, which is the default, so
  // this entry takes precedence over ASPNETCORE_URLS and ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS and Kestrel logs an
  // override warning if either is also set. The parent Dockerfile should therefore NOT set those
  // variables for this service; the address is fixed here. The override channel that DOES work is
  // the same configuration key from the environment - Kestrel__Endpoints__Default__Url - because the
  // environment provider is added after this file and wins on an identical key. VERIFIED, not
  // assumed.
  //
  // `+` binds every interface, which is required: a container reached by service name cannot bind
  // loopback only.
  "Kestrel": {
    "Endpoints": {
      // ONE endpoint, both protocol versions, selected per connection by ALPN: HTTP/2 for the gRPC
      // contracts C-03 and C-04, HTTP/1.1 for the anonymous /health probe, the authenticated
      // /v1/ping and the thin REST projection of C-03 and C-04 that Gateway consumes.
      "Default": {
        "Url": "https://+:5102",
        "Protocols": "Http1AndHttp2"
      }
    }
  },

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // INBOUND BEARER VALIDATION - binds JwtAuthenticationOptions ("Authentication:Jwt").
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // VERIFICATION ONLY. THERE IS NO SIGNING MATERIAL IN THIS SECTION AND THERE NEVER MAY BE.
  // Security is the sole issuer in this system: exactly one signing value exists anywhere in it and
  // Security holds it. This service verifies and never mints, which is why the project file
  // deliberately omits the token-minting package - the capability is not merely unused here, it is
  // unreachable. Security's signing variable name does not appear anywhere in this file, because a
  // service that has no reason to name it has no reason to be able to read it (C-G).
  //
  // The stock bearer handler does all the work from the two values below: it fetches the discovery
  // document beneath the authority, retrieves the published key set, and then performs signature
  // checking, key rollover, issuer and audience validation and clock-skew tolerance by itself. That
  // is why Security is REST rather than gRPC - the security-critical path becomes framework code
  // instead of hand-written code, in three services rather than one.
  //
  // THIS SECTION SITS AT THE TOP LEVEL, NOT UNDER `DataServices`. JwtAuthenticationOptions declares
  // its section as "Authentication:Jwt" by ASP.NET Core convention. Nesting it would move the path
  // to "DataServices:Authentication:Jwt" and bind nothing.
  "Authentication": {
    "Jwt": {
      // The Security service, reached by service name on port 5104 inside the orchestration
      // network. The discovery document and the published key set are resolved BENEATH this
      // address, so it is a base address: the startup validator rejects a value carrying embedded
      // credentials, a query string or a fragment, and rejects any scheme other than http or https.
      //
      // WHY THE SCHEME IS https. Every internal listener in this system is TLS-terminated - see this
      // file's Kestrel section for the measured protocol-multiplexing reason that applies to this
      // service and to Persistence - but Security's is the one whose TLS requirement would stand even
      // if it served a single protocol version, and for a functional reason rather than a hardening
      // preference. Three already-published artifacts say so and agree with each other:
      //
      //   * docs/ARCHITECTURE.md 4.1 marks port 5104 "over HTTPS" - the only row in the port map so
      //     marked - and instructs consumers to configure their bearer handler's authority as an
      //     https address with metadata retrieval over HTTPS required, using the service name on
      //     the same port inside the orchestration network. That is literally this key.
      //   * shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/OpenApi/security.v1.yaml publishes exactly one server
      //     entry and it is https, with the reasoning attached: token issuance authenticates the
      //     caller with a CLIENT CERTIFICATE, and a client certificate cannot be presented on a
      //     plaintext listener at all - so over http the token endpoint would be uncallable and no
      //     service could obtain its first credential. It also records that a plaintext probe
      //     belongs in a development overlay and never in a published base URL.
      //   * The same document identifies these endpoints as the system's TRUST BOOTSTRAP. Every
      //     other service verifies against the key set published here. Fetched over a channel an
      //     attacker can rewrite, a substituted key set makes all three other services accept
      //     tokens the attacker signed, and each of them would be behaving exactly as designed
      //     while doing it. No correctness elsewhere recovers from that.
      //
      // THE DEVELOPMENT OVERLAY RETARGETS THIS AT LOOPBACK BUT KEEPS THE SCHEME. It sets
      // https://localhost:5104 and does NOT relax RequireHttpsMetadata, because relaxing it would
      // buy nothing once the authority is https - the setting only PERMITS a plaintext metadata
      // address, it does not make one exist. A deployment whose Security instance genuinely listens
      // on plaintext can still relax both without a code change by overriding
      // Authentication__Jwt__Authority and Authentication__Jwt__RequireHttpsMetadata from the
      // environment, but it does so knowing it has moved the system's trust bootstrap onto a channel
      // an attacker on path can rewrite.
      "Authority": "https://security-service:5104",

      // Null means DERIVE the discovery address from the authority above, which is correct whenever
      // the authority is addressed directly - and it is, here. The key is written rather than
      // omitted so the derivation is visible in the configuration document rather than being an
      // invisible default, and so the reconciliation against DataServicesOptions.cs has no orphan
      // in either direction.
      //
      // MEASURED: a JSON null binds this property to null and not to an empty string, so the
      // startup validator's present-but-blank rule does not fire. That distinction matters, because
      // an EMPTY string here is rejected on purpose: an unset property means "derive it", whereas a
      // property written and then left blank means a configuration entry was begun and not
      // finished, which is a mistake rather than a choice. Supply a value only for a topology where
      // the discovery document is not beneath the authority - and if you do, it must be an absolute
      // http or https address. It may carry a query string, unlike the authority, because it is a
      // complete document address rather than a base onto which paths are composed.
      "MetadataAddress": null,

      // This service's own identity in the audience set Security mints against. A credential minted
      // for another service is refused, which is the entire point of validating the audience: a
      // token is never valid somewhere its holder did not intend, and the issuance contract carries
      // one audience per request precisely so that holds.
      //
      // The spelling follows the only committed precedent for an AUDIENCE value in this repository,
      // which is the Gateway service's own. Note that the bare token "dataservices" also appears in
      // the repository, in the upstream-health enumeration of the gateway contract - that is a
      // SERVICE NAME, not an audience, and the two must not be conflated.
      "Audience": "powerframework-dataservices",

      // Required, and true, and consistent with the https authority above - a deliberate,
      // overridable posture rather than an oversight, which is why it is a key and not a compiled-in
      // decision.
      //
      // The startup validator enforces the relationship in ONE DIRECTION ONLY, and the direction is
      // the point: requiring transport-secured metadata while pointing at a plaintext authority is
      // a contradiction the handler would discover only on its first fetch, so that combination is
      // refused at startup. A plaintext authority with this switch OFF is legitimate and is
      // deliberately allowed, because it is the shape a loopback developer run takes.
      //
      // A deployment that must relax it therefore sets it to false alongside a plaintext authority,
      // from the environment or from the development overlay - never here. Keeping the secure value
      // in the BASE settings is what makes any relaxation visible as an explicit act by the
      // environment that asked for it, rather than an estate-wide default nobody chose.
      "RequireHttpsMetadata": true,

      // The four validation switches, all true. NONE MAY BE WEAKENED. C-G requires every newly
      // created boundary to be genuinely authenticated, and each false here would turn this edge
      // into a boundary that merely looks authenticated:
      //   ValidateIssuer           - a token from any issuer would be accepted, which defeats the
      //                              sole-issuer topology in a single setting.
      //   ValidateAudience         - a token minted for another service would be accepted here.
      //   ValidateLifetime         - Security mints SHORT-LIVED credentials; this switch is what
      //                              makes the shortness mean anything at all.
      //   ValidateIssuerSigningKey - a switch, not a value: it selects whether the signature is
      //                              verified against the material fetched from the authority at
      //                              runtime. It is the only key name in this file containing the
      //                              word "key", it holds no material, and being a boolean it could
      //                              not.
      "ValidateIssuer": true,
      "ValidateAudience": true,
      "ValidateLifetime": true,
      "ValidateIssuerSigningKey": true
    }
  },

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // THE SERVICE'S OWN SETTINGS - binds DataServicesOptions ("DataServices").
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // TWO KINDS OF VALUE FOLLOW, AND THE LINE BETWEEN THEM IS THE MOST IMPORTANT THING IN THIS FILE.
  //
  //   PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULTS - Localization, RowSelect, ContextMenu, ColumnExpression and
  //   DropDownSearch. Every value in those five groups is a behaviour-preservation claim under C-B,
  //   read at a named line of ws_objects/** and reproduced exactly. The defect being remediated is
  //   the HARDCODING, not the value: each is preserved as the default and made overridable. A value
  //   off by one here is a silent behavioural regression that no unit test would flag AS one - it
  //   would simply record the wrong behaviour as correct.
  //
  //   BOUNDARY-CREATED SETTINGS - Persistence, Security, Resilience, Sessions and EventChain. Those
  //   five groups have NO legacy locator and their absence is not an omission. PowerFramework was a
  //   library with no process of its own, no listener and no server tier, so it had no network
  //   address to configure, no transport failure to handle, no server-held session to expire and no
  //   delivery order to enforce across a wire. Each group is labelled as boundary-created at its own
  //   heading so nobody later hunts for the legacy line it came from.
  "DataServices": {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT 1 of 5 - localization.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    "Localization": {
      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT. ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L94 reads `lang = "en"`, sitting
      // directly beneath the multi-language-support comment at :L93, inside the framework
      // application's open event.
      //
      // Exactly three values are accepted, from the provider selection at pfw.sra:L95-L102: "en"
      // selects the English provider, "chs" the Simplified Chinese provider - a genuine no-op,
      // because Simplified Chinese is the base locale - and "cht" the Traditional Chinese provider.
      // The startup validator compares ORDINALLY, because PowerScript's choose case on strings is
      // case-sensitive, so the legacy would not have matched a differently cased spelling either.
      //
      // DataServices needs its own installed provider rather than inheriting one: the localization
      // surface is called from inside this service's own validation path, with the DataWindow
      // service category, so an uninstalled provider here would land those lookups in the silent
      // passthrough fallback and emit untranslated text on a live validation error.
      //
      // ONE LEGACY DETAIL IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPRODUCED, and the distinction matters. That
      // selection has no `case else` arm, so an unrecognised locale left the provider null and
      // installed nothing. Here an unrecognised value is refused at startup, because Program.cs
      // must map this string onto a concrete provider and a value it cannot map is a structural
      // configuration fault rather than a behaviour worth imitating. The silent passthrough itself
      // is preserved where it belongs, inside the localization surface when no provider is
      // installed at all.
      "Locale": "en"
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT 2 of 5 - row selection.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    "RowSelect": {
      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT.
      // ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/n_cst_dwsvc_rowselect.sru:L25 declares
      // `privatewrite long #Style = RS_SINGLE`, and the two style constants sit immediately above
      // it: RS_SINGLE = 1 at :L20 and RS_MULTIPLE = 2 at :L21. The default is therefore 1.
      //
      // IT IS A COMBINABLE BITMASK RATHER THAN A TWO-VALUED CHOICE. The comment at :L19 states that
      // the style supports combination, so a value may legitimately carry both bits and 3 is a
      // meaningful setting. Written as the numeric literal because the RS_* constants are declared
      // on the legacy service object itself and are absent from the framework-wide constant
      // catalogue, so in the .NET tree they belong to the row-select service rather than to
      // configuration.
      //
      // ZERO IS REFUSED AT STARTUP, and that is evidenced rather than invented: :L169 reads
      // `if style = 0 then return RetCode.E_INVALID_ARGUMENT`. The guard is deliberately NOT
      // mirrored onto DropDownSearch:FilterType below, whose legacy setter validates nothing at
      // all - see the note there. The asymmetry between the two is legacy behaviour and must not be
      // harmonised in either direction.
      "Style": 1
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT 3 of 5 - which context-menu commands the model offers.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // ALL FIVE ARE true. Locators, one per key:
    // ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/n_cst_dwsvc_contextmenu.sru:L49 through :L53, each
    // declared `boolean ... = true`. All five are plain `public` rather than `privatewrite`, so the
    // hosting application could assign them directly; plain settable keys reproduce that faithfully.
    //
    // THESE CONFIGURE THE HEADLESS HALF ONLY. The plan splits that legacy object: the menu ITEM
    // MODEL - labels, identifiers, enabled and split flags, and computed LOGICAL text widths - ships
    // in this service and crosses the contract, while window geometry, scale conversion, font
    // measurement and the actual rendering are deferred and named as a reserved Gateway extension
    // point. Each key decides whether a command appears in the model; none says anything about how
    // it is drawn, and no key here may ever acquire a pixel, font or scale meaning (C-D).
    //
    // The reserved menu identifiers at :L37-L45 are deliberately absent. They are the menu model's
    // own constants, not settings, and a constant no deployment may vary is not configuration.
    "ContextMenu": {
      // :L49 - `boolean #ColAutoWidth = true`. Automatic column width. Governs whether the command
      // is offered; the width computation the model carries is logical, so nothing about this key
      // reaches the deferred rendering half.
      "ColAutoWidth": true,
      // :L50 - `boolean #ColCheck = true`. Check and clear-check of a whole column. ONE flag governs
      // BOTH commands, exactly as the legacy does; splitting it into two keys would be a new
      // capability rather than a preserved one.
      "ColCheck": true,
      // :L51 - `boolean #ColCopy = true`. Copy a whole column's values.
      "ColCopy": true,
      // :L52 - `boolean #ColPaste = true`. Overwrite a whole column from clipboard data.
      "ColPaste": true,
      // :L53 - `boolean #ItemCopy = true`. Copy a single cell's value.
      "ItemCopy": true
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT 4 of 5 - the column-expression engine.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // From ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/n_cst_dwsvc_columnexp.sru, at 2,435 lines the
    // largest in-scope legacy object. Only the values that object exposes as VARYING STATE appear
    // here. Its grammar sentinels, its tri-state calculation cache and its variable-kind
    // discriminators are engine internals and are deliberately absent: a value no deployment may
    // vary is not a setting.
    "ColumnExpression": {
      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT. :L98 declares `privatewrite boolean #Trace` with NO initializer,
      // so the PowerBuilder default is false and tracing off is legacy behaviour. The setter is
      // of_settrace at :L2409, and the consumption site is :L752 - when set, the engine walks its
      // calculation stack to build a call-stack string and raises the trace event carrying the
      // stack, the expression and the evaluated value.
      //
      // TURNING THIS ON BY DEFAULT WOULD BE A BEHAVIOURAL CHANGE DRESSED AS A PREFERENCE (C-B), and
      // it would also put caller-supplied expression text into the trace stream by default, which
      // the redaction obligation noted on the Logging section above speaks directly to.
      "Trace": false,

      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT, AND THE NUMBER IS EXACTLY 200. :L223 and :L225 are a matched pair
      // bracketing the cascade dispatch at :L224, inside the item-changed event spanning
      // :L211-L227: the row count is read at :L222, redraw is switched off before the cascade when
      // that count exceeds 200, and switched back on afterwards under the identical condition.
      //
      // THE COMPARISON IS STRICTLY GREATER-THAN AND THE BOUNDARY IS OBSERVABLE. At exactly 200 rows
      // there is NO suppression. A consumer must compare rowCount > threshold and never
      // rowCount >= threshold; the inclusive form moves the boundary by one row at precisely the
      // value the legacy names.
      //
      // This is a reproduced legacy behaviour with an observable boundary and is deliberately not
      // described as anything else. It is NOT a tuning knob and NOT a performance setting - no such
      // objective is published anywhere in the repository, so none may be asserted here. Zero is a
      // legal value and means every non-empty cascade suppresses redraw.
      "RedrawSuppressionRowThreshold": 200,

      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT. :L2421-L2422 is the engine's constructor: it creates the vector
      // held at :L110 and then reserves 20 entries in it.
      //
      // The stack this capacity belongs to is not hidden behind the boundary - its contents are what
      // the trace event's call-stack string is built from at :L752-L757, so the ORDERING it
      // preserves is observable on contract C-04. The capacity itself is not observable, because it
      // is a starting allocation and the stack grows past it, which is why it is configurable rather
      // than fixed. Must be positive: a reserved capacity is a size, and zero would express no
      // intent while discarding the one number the legacy states.
      "CalcStackInitialCapacity": 20
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT 5 of 5 - drop-down search. THE MOST HAZARDOUS GROUP IN THIS FILE.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // From ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/n_cst_dwsvc_dropdownsearch.sru. The plan
    // splits that object too: filter-expression construction, the search state machine and the row
    // and filtered counts ship here, while window positioning and text-input-method handling are
    // deferred and named as a reserved Gateway extension point. All three keys belong to the
    // headless half.
    //
    // TWO UNRELATED LEGACY VALUES IN THIS ONE OBJECT ARE BOTH THE NUMBER SEVEN, and confusing them
    // is a silent behavioural change. FilterType is 3 and PinyinMatchFlags is 7. Read both notes
    // before altering either.
    "DropDownSearch": {
      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT, AND IT IS 3 - NOT 7. :L57 declares
      // `privatewrite ulong #FilterType = FILTER_DISP + FILTER_DISP_PY`. The four constants above it
      // are FILTER_DISP = 1 at :L50 (match the display column), FILTER_DISP_PY = 2 at :L51 (match
      // the display column by the pinyin first letters of the input), FILTER_DATA = 4 at :L52 (match
      // the data column) and FILTER_ALL at :L53, which is their sum. So the default is 1 | 2 = 3,
      // because THE LEGACY DELIBERATELY OMITS THE DATA-COLUMN BIT.
      //
      // DO NOT "COMPLETE" THIS TO 7. FILTER_ALL is 7, and setting this key to 7 would enable a
      // data-column match the legacy does not perform by default - a new behaviour rather than a
      // preserved one (C-B). The numeral 7 appears legitimately in this group exactly once, on
      // PinyinMatchFlags below, which is an unrelated contract that merely shares the digit.
      //
      // The value is combinable - the comment at :L49 says so - and the three bits are tested
      // independently at :L318, :L321 and :L326, each guarding one clause of the constructed filter
      // expression.
      //
      // THE STARTUP VALIDATOR DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CHECK THIS KEY, AND THE OMISSION IS THE POINT.
      // The legacy setter at :L304-L305 is two statements - assign the argument, return success -
      // with no validation whatsoever, so zero is accepted and simply produces an empty filter.
      // A non-zero or range guard would add behaviour the legacy does not have, which C-B forbids as
      // firmly as it forbids removing behaviour. That is the exact inverse of RowSelect:Style above,
      // whose legacy setter does guard zero.
      "FilterType": 3,

      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT, AND THE null IS THE DEFAULT - NOT false. :L58 declares
      // `privatewrite boolean #ShowFilteredRows` with no initializer, and its comment continued at
      // :L59 ends by stating that a NULL value means automatic determination and that this is the
      // default. The null is explicit rather than incidental: the constructor at :L503 consists of
      // nothing but a super call and a call setting this very field to null.
      //
      // THE null GATES A REAL THREE-BRANCH ALGORITHM, WHICH IS WHY IT MUST STAY null. The resolver
      // at :L260 opens with an explicit-value short circuit, and only when the field is null does it
      // evaluate :L261-L263 in order: if the display and data columns are the same column, false;
      // otherwise if the presentation style is grid, true; otherwise the conjunction of more than
      // one row and a visible vertical scroll bar.
      //
      // So writing false here would not merely change a default - IT WOULD DELETE THAT ALGORITHM,
      // because false and "decide for me" would become indistinguishable. The property is bound as a
      // nullable boolean, and the startup validator neither checks nor normalises it, deliberately.
      // MEASURED: a JSON null binds it to no-value rather than to false, so the three states survive
      // the round trip through configuration intact.
      "ShowFilteredRows": null,

      // PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULT, AND THIS IS THE ONE VALUE IN THIS GROUP THAT REALLY IS 7. :L323
      // appends a pinyin first-letter clause to the constructed filter with the flags argument
      // written as the literal 7. That clause is reached only when both guards above it hold: the
      // pinyin bit of FilterType is set, tested at :L321, and the input matches an alphabetic
      // pattern, tested at :L322.
      //
      // The 7 decomposes into named constants that DO live in the framework-wide catalogue, at
      // ws_objects/pfw.shared.pbl.src/enums.sru:L1147-L1149 - ignore case is 1, ignore full-width
      // versus half-width forms is 2, and match fuzzy pronunciation is 4 - and their sum is exactly
      // the 7 the call site hardcodes.
      //
      // THE FLAGS ARE NOT THE UNKNOWN PART. What no flag value reveals is the character-to-first-
      // letter mapping and the fuzzy-sound groupings, which exist only inside the closed native
      // binary. The matcher is therefore characterized against the behavioural oracle or reported
      // BLOCKED, and is never approximated - so this key configures the documented half of that
      // capability and no more. Its whole domain is legal at the call site, so the validator does
      // not constrain it.
      "PinyinMatchFlags": 7
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // BOUNDARY-CREATED 1 of 5 - where to reach Persistence. NO legacy equivalent.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    "Persistence": {
      // The gRPC base address of the Persistence service, consumed by Clients/PersistenceClient.cs
      // to construct the channel carrying contracts C-05 through C-08 - query, update, command and
      // transaction. The service name is how it is reached inside the orchestration network; the
      // development overlay retargets this at loopback.
      //
      // THE PORT IS 5101 - THE ONE THE PORT MAP FIXES - AND THE SCHEME IS WHAT MAKES THAT WORK.
      // Persistence faces the identical constraint this service does and answers it the identical
      // way: ONE endpoint, `https://+:5101` with `Protocols "Http1AndHttp2"`, carrying contracts
      // C-05 through C-08 over HTTP/2 and the anonymous /health plus the authenticated /v1/ping over
      // HTTP/1.1, with ALPN selecting the version per connection. A cleartext `http://…:5101` here
      // fails every retrieval and every update with the HTTP/2 error `HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED` during
      // transport negotiation, before the request reaches Persistence, so nothing on the far side
      // logs the cause. An earlier revision of this file answered the constraint with a second,
      // undeclared h2c port outside the fixed 5101-5105 band; that band is the one the attached
      // environment fixes and its only spare slot is the reserved 5103, so the parallel band was
      // withdrawn in favour of TLS on the assigned port. orchestration/.env.example supplies this
      // value as PERSISTENCE_BASE_URL, which the readiness probes read as well - one listener, one
      // address.
      //
      // CREATED BY THE DECOMPOSITION. In the legacy library this was an in-process method call with
      // no address to configure at all. It became a network call when the boundary was drawn between
      // the DataWindow service layer and the only component permitted to generate or execute SQL.
      //
      // THE SCHEME IS https AND THAT IS FUNCTIONAL RATHER THAN A HARDENING PREFERENCE. gRPC requires
      // HTTP/2, and Persistence serves gRPC and its HTTP/1.1 readiness probe on the SAME port 5101,
      // which is only possible when ALPN can negotiate between them - measured on this toolchain, a
      // cleartext endpoint configured for both versions disables HTTP/2 outright and every gRPC call
      // fails. See this file's Kestrel section for the measurement and for the identical constraint
      // on this service's own listener. A caller that named http here would be pointed at a scheme
      // Persistence does not serve, which is exactly the class of defect this address must not
      // reintroduce. The startup validator enforces the shape: absolute, http or https, no embedded
      // credentials, no query string, no fragment. AN ADDRESS IS NEVER A PLACE TO PUT A SECRET - the
      // userinfo rejection is a secrets control (C-F), because such a value would be a credential
      // filed under a key named for an address and would be copied into every log record, exception
      // and trace that captures the request URI.
      //
      // ONLY AN ADDRESS APPEARS HERE. No upstream schema, internal type or configuration value of
      // the Persistence service is represented anywhere in this file; the wire shape travels
      // exclusively through the generated contract assembly (C-A). And there is deliberately NO
      // connection string, NO data-source key and NO storage setting of any kind in this file -
      // Persistence is the only service in the refactor that holds a storage provider (C-E).
      "Address": "https://persistence-service:5101"
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // BOUNDARY-CREATED 2 of 5 - where to reach Security. NO legacy equivalent.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    "Security": {
      // The REST base address of the Security service, consumed by Clients/SecurityClient.cs for
      // contract C-02, the cryptographic surface. Security is REST rather than gRPC because its
      // interface is stateless request/response with nothing to stream and no ordering requirement -
      // and because its published verification material and discovery document must be reachable by
      // the framework's stock handlers with no bespoke retrieval code.
      //
      // CREATED BY THE DECOMPOSITION. The legacy cryptographic surface was an in-process object in
      // the same address space, so it had no address either. It became a network call when Security
      // became the sole holder of the keyed cryptographic surface and the sole issuer of credentials.
      //
      // https, for the same three reasons recorded on Authentication:Jwt:Authority above, and the
      // same port. Kept as a separate key from the authority rather than derived from it because the
      // two are different concerns - one is the crypto API this service calls, the other is the
      // authority its inbound-credential handler trusts - and a deployment must be able to move
      // either without the other.
      //
      // THIS IS AN ADDRESS ONLY. It carries no credential and no key material, and no key in this
      // file could hold either: under contract C-02 raw key material never crosses the wire from a
      // caller, which passes an opaque reference that Security resolves on its own side.
      "BaseAddress": "https://security-service:5104",

      // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      // THE CLIENT IDENTITY THIS SERVICE PRESENTS ON THE TOKEN-ISSUANCE EDGE.
      // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      //
      // WHY THIS GROUP EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS FUNCTIONAL RATHER THAN HARDENING. Every one of the
      // seventeen C-02 operations requires a bearer credential, and the only way to obtain one is
      // POST /v1/tokens on the address above - which is protected by MUTUAL TLS and by nothing else,
      // because A CALLER CANNOT PRESENT A BEARER TOKEN IN ORDER TO OBTAIN ITS FIRST BEARER TOKEN.
      // Without a client certificate to present, this service obtains no credential and every
      // cryptographic call it would make is unreachable.
      //
      // TWO PATHS, AND THERE IS NOWHERE HERE TO PUT MATERIAL. Both values name files mounted from the
      // orchestration secret layer - GATEWAY and DATASERVICES each have their own pair in
      // orchestration/.env.example. There is no certificate body, no key body and no passphrase key
      // in this group, in this file, or on the bound options type, so C-F cannot be violated by
      // filling one in: there is no such key to fill.
      //
      // BOTH EMPTY IS A VALID STATE and is the default: it means this deployment presents no client
      // certificate and requests no token. Setting one without the other is refused at startup with a
      // named error, because a certificate cannot complete a handshake without its key and a key has
      // nothing to present without its certificate.
      "MutualTls": {
        "CertificatePath": "",
        "CertificateKeyPath": ""
      }
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // BOUNDARY-CREATED 3 of 5 - transport-failure handling for the two typed clients.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // CREATED BY THE DECOMPOSITION, AND IT EXISTS FOR EXACTLY ONE REASON: AN IN-PROCESS CALL CANNOT
    // FAIL IN TRANSIT AND A NETWORK CALL CAN. Every call these settings cover was an in-process
    // method call in the legacy library, so the failure mode did not exist and there was nothing to
    // configure. Handling a failure mode that the decomposition itself creates is required BY the
    // transition rather than layered on top of it: without it the first transient transport fault
    // would surface as a defect the legacy could not have had, which is a regression introduced by
    // the refactor rather than a preserved behaviour.
    //
    // THESE ARE FAILURE-HANDLING SETTINGS AND NOTHING ELSE. The repository publishes no
    // service-level agreement, no latency budget, no throughput target and no availability
    // commitment, so no value below may be read, tuned or justified as a performance objective.
    //
    // Every value restates the resilience library's own standard-handler default rather than
    // inventing a number. Restating them is the whole point of the group: it makes each one visible
    // in the configuration document and overridable per environment. The two clients are configured
    // separately because they are different services over different transports - the shape is
    // shared, the values need not be. Durations use the ordinary time-span text form, so an
    // environment override needs no bespoke converter.
    //
    // There is deliberately NO backoff-type key: the options type exposes no such member, so one
    // here would bind to nothing. The backoff shape is chosen where the pipelines are built, in
    // Clients/PersistenceClient.cs and Clients/SecurityClient.cs.
    "Resilience": {
      "Persistence": {
        // Retries of a failed attempt. Zero is legal and means one attempt with an immediately
        // surfaced failure, which is a policy rather than a misconfiguration.
        "MaxRetryAttempts": 3,
        // The base delay the backoff between attempts is derived from. Must be positive.
        "RetryBaseDelay": "00:00:02",
        // The proportion of failed attempts in one sampling window that opens the circuit. Greater
        // than zero and at most one; zero is refused rather than read as "never open", because the
        // underlying strategy refuses it too and a setting that faults pipeline construction is
        // exactly the structural fault startup validation exists to catch.
        "CircuitBreakerFailureRatio": 0.1,
        // The minimum number of attempts that must be OBSERVED in one window before the proportion
        // is considered at all - a proportion computed from a single observation carries no
        // information. A count of observed attempts, not a rate and not a claim about anything this
        // service achieves. The name mirrors the underlying strategy member one-to-one on purpose.
        "CircuitBreakerMinimumThroughput": 100,
        // The window over which attempts are sampled. Must be positive.
        "CircuitBreakerSamplingDuration": "00:00:30",
        // How long the circuit stays open before attempts are let through again. Must be positive.
        "CircuitBreakerBreakDuration": "00:00:05",
        // The ceiling on one whole request, retries included, after which it is abandoned. A
        // failure-handling bound rather than a target: it decides when an unanswered call is treated
        // as failed instead of waited on forever, a decision the legacy never had to make because an
        // in-process call always returned. Must be positive.
        "RequestTimeout": "00:00:30"
      },
      "Security": {
        // Same shape, configured independently. See the annotations on the Persistence block above;
        // they apply member for member.
        "MaxRetryAttempts": 3,
        "RetryBaseDelay": "00:00:02",
        "CircuitBreakerFailureRatio": 0.1,
        "CircuitBreakerMinimumThroughput": 100,
        "CircuitBreakerSamplingDuration": "00:00:30",
        "CircuitBreakerBreakDuration": "00:00:05",
        "RequestTimeout": "00:00:30"
      }
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // BOUNDARY-CREATED 4 of 5 - lifetimes for the two server-held session kinds.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // CREATED BY THE DECOMPOSITION, and the reason is structural rather than an omission. In the
    // legacy library both kinds of state lived inside an object whose lifetime was the hosting
    // control's, so neither needed a lifetime of its own:
    //
    //   * The validation chain's cross-event state is four private fields on the DataWindow service
    //     extension at ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/se_cst_dw.sru:L89-L96 - the
    //     disabled-event mask at :L89, the two re-entrancy flags at :L92 and :L94, and at :L96 the
    //     item-changed result STASHED FOR THE VALIDATION-ERROR EVENT TO CONSUME.
    //   * The expression engine's cross-DataWindow references are live object pointers, declared at
    //     ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/n_cst_dwsvc_columnexp.sru:L80-L83.
    //
    // A stateless request boundary has nowhere to put either, so both become server-held state
    // correlated by an identifier and opened and closed by dedicated calls - and anything
    // server-held and correlated by an identifier needs a lifetime, because a client that never
    // closes its session must not retain it forever. THE KEYS EXIST BECAUSE OF THE BOUNDARY, NOT
    // BECAUSE ANY BEHAVIOUR CHANGED.
    //
    // Both values are therefore starting points for a deployment rather than reproductions of a
    // legacy number, since no legacy number exists to reproduce. Both are validated positive: a zero
    // idle timeout would expire a session the instant it opened, and a zero ceiling would refuse
    // every session.
    //
    // NOTE THE NESTING. These two groups sit under DataServices:Sessions, not at the DataServices
    // root - the options type is a Sessions group containing them. DataServices:ValidationSession
    // would bind nothing and leave the real values on their compiled-in defaults.
    "Sessions": {
      "ValidationSession": {
        // How long a validation session may sit without a call before it is closed and its four
        // pieces of cross-event state released.
        "IdleTimeout": "00:05:00",
        // How many validation sessions may be open at once. An admission bound rather than a target:
        // it decides when a further session is refused outright, a decision the legacy never had to
        // make because its sessions were objects in the caller's own address space.
        "MaxConcurrentSessions": 100
      },
      "ExpressionSession": {
        // Same reasoning for expression sessions, which scope the DataWindow handles that
        // cross-DataWindow expression variables resolve through. That scoping is what makes those
        // variables expressible across a boundary at all, and its limit is deliberate and
        // documented: a reference is supported only while both DataWindows are co-resident in one
        // session, and a reference reaching outside one is refused with a defined error rather than
        // answered with a guess.
        "IdleTimeout": "00:05:00",
        "MaxConcurrentSessions": 100
      }
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // BOUNDARY-CREATED 5 of 5 - event-chain ordering.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // CREATED BY THE DECOMPOSITION. In the legacy library the 22-event chain was dispatched
    // in-process by the runtime and COULD NOT ARRIVE OUT OF ORDER AT ALL, so the question this group
    // answers only becomes askable once the chain crosses a wire.
    "EventChain": {
      // true, and there is no lenient mode on purpose. The item-change and validation chain is
      // assigned the strictly synchronous ordering pattern, under which the monotonic sequence
      // numbers on the stream exist FOR DETECTION ONLY: an out-of-order arrival is a HARD ERROR and
      // never a reorder opportunity.
      //
      // Reordering that group is not merely undesirable, it is semantically impossible, which is why
      // the default is what it is. The validation-error handler READS AND CLEARS the result stashed
      // by the item-change event that precedes it and pre-sets its own outcome from that value
      // [se_cst_dw.sru:L322-L385]; the item-change handler fires a NESTED event from inside itself
      // [:L182-L253]; and the kill-focus handler queues a deferred accept only when the item-change
      // re-entrancy flag is clear [:L387-L393]. Each is a function of the preceding event's outcome,
      // so delivering them in a different order would not produce a late result - it would produce a
      // wrong one.
      //
      // The setting exists rather than being compiled in because the notification-shaped events of
      // the chain - focus, mouse and row-focus - are assigned the sequencing-token pattern instead,
      // and a deployment must be able to state which posture its consumers implement without a
      // rebuild.
      "StrictOrdering": true
    }
  }
}
